+/// itself.
+///
+/// The platform draws that area through the presentation, and it never dismisses
+/// the sheet on a tap there: the area belongs to the presentation, not to the
+/// app's view tree, so nothing on the React Native side sees the tap either. This
+/// view sits inside the presented content. It installs one tap recognizer on the
+/// presentation's own container, accepts only taps that land outside the sheet's
+/// own frame, and reports them. The sheet stays presented, because the caller
+/// owns the decision (a form may ask before it discards a draft).
